1. variables.tf — Define the input
************************************
Declare the variable name, data type, default value (if any), constraints, and validation rules.

Example: VM size must be a string.

2. prod_terraform.tfvars — Supply the value
************************************
Provide the actual value you want to use for that environment.

Example: vm_size = "Standard_D2s_v5"

3. main.tf — Use the value
************************************
Reference the variable inside a resource using var.variable_name.

Example: size = var.vm_size

Let's start One One Example
***********************************
String : 
***********
variables.tf >>>

variable "location" {
  type = string
}

terraform.tfvars >> location = "Central India"
main.tf > location = var.location

Number: An integer or decimal number. 
***********
variables.tf >
variable "disk_size_gb" {
  type = number
}

terraform.tfvars > disk_size_gb = 128
main.tf > disk_size_gb = var.disk_size_gb

Bol : Only true or false. 
*********
variables.tf
variable "enable_backup" {
  type = bool
}

terraform.tfvars > enable_backup = true
main.tf > enabled = var.enable_backup

List : Ordered collection; values have the same element type. 
*********************************
variables.tf

variable "allowed_ports" {
  type = list(number)
}

terraform.tfvars > allowed_ports = [80, 443, 8080]
main.tf > ports = var.allowed_ports

Set : Unique values; order is not guaranteed. 
********************************************
variables.tf >
variable "allowed_regions" {
  type = set(string)
}

terraform.tfvars > allowed_regions = ["Central India", "East US"]
main.tf > regions = var.allowed_regions

Map : Key-value pairs with the same value type. 
*****************************************************
variables.tf >
variable "tags" {
  type = map(string)
}

terraform.tfvars > tags = { Environment = "Prod", Team = "Cloud" }
main.tf > tags = var.tags

Object : A structured value with named attributes and defined types. 
*****************************************************************************
variables.tf >

variable "vm_config" {
  type = object({
    name = string
    size = string
    disk = number
  })
}

terraform.tfvars >

vm_config = {
  name = "vm-prod-01"
  size = "Standard_D2s_v5"
  disk = 128
}

main.tf >
name = var.vm_config.name
size = var.vm_config.size

Tupple : An ordered collection with a specific type for each position. 
******************************************************************************
variables.tf >

variable "vm_details" {
  type = tuple([string, number, bool])
}

terraform.tfvars > vm_details = ["vm-prod-01", 128, true]
main.tf > name = var.vm_details[0]

Constraints — how to restrict inputs .. Normally we write condition in variable.tf with variable block
*****************************************************************************
Constraints are restrictions that prevent users from providing unwanted values. 
In Terraform, you commonly enforce them with validation, numeric bounds, nullable, and resource or provider-specific arguments.

Ex : variables.tf >

variable "location" {
  type        = string
  description = "Azure deployment region"

  validation {
    condition = contains(
      ["Central India", "East US"],
      var.location
    )
    error_message = "Location must be Central India or East US."
  }
}

Rest other .tfvar and ,main normally stays same.
